ulating and indicating device variant — \"Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\"). Hmm, that's a type designation mentioned. Should I include \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" in type_designations? It's mentioned in revision history as a variant of the calculating/indicating device. It could be included. But careful not to fabricate context. I'll include certified_type.category \"Water meter\" and maybe omit type_designations since the fragment doesn't clearly state the certified type designation. Actually the revision 9 entry names \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" as a variant of calculating and indicating device. Including it as a type designation might be defensible but risky. I'll include it — it's a concrete type identifier mentioned. Hmm, but it's a component variant (calculating and indicating device), not the whole certified type. Maybe better as a component? The fragment doesn't describe components structurally. I'll leave certified_type minimal: category \"Water meter\". Actually, let me include type_designations with \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"? I think it's cleaner to omit type_designations and mention in... no, schema has no free-text spot except description. I could set certified_type.description null/omit. I'll include components? Not clearly named as components in this fragment (just revision history). Rule 6: include components only if cert names sub-assemblies. Revision 9 mentions \"calculating and indicating device\" variant AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z and revision 10 mentions \"U200 wireless module\". These are named sub-assemblies. I could add components entries:\n- {role: \"calculating and indicating device\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]} — as a Mobile comms variant.\n- {role: \"wireless module\", type_designations: [\"U200\"]}\n\nThese come from revision history descriptions, which is legitimate content of the cert. I'll include them, cautiously. Hmm, but that might be over-extraction. The revision history says \"Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\" — this indicates the cert includes that variant. And \"change to U200 wireless module for global use\". I'll include components entries. It adds value.\n\n- model_family: family_name: \"AquaMaster\"? Not stated in fragment (AquaMaster4 appears in rev 9). Hmm, family name — the devices are \"AquaMaster\" series but the fragment only shows \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\". I'd set family_name null to avoid fabrication. Models: union of DN sizes across the tables: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800.\n\nLet me enumerate:\n- Reduced bore AC: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\n- Reduced bore battery: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\n- Full bore AC: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800.\n- Full bore battery: DN40–DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650.\n\nUnion: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800.\n\nThat's 29 models. Should each model entry include attributes like bore type availability and power supply options? I could add attributes per model, e.g. {\"bore\": [\"reduced\",\"full\"], \"power_supply\": [...]}. That might be heavy but informative. The schema allows attributes{}. Let me build:\n\nFor each DN size:\n- reduced_bore: available with AC and battery (both tables 9 and 10 cover DN40–DN600 fully). Yes reduced bore covers DN40–DN600 in both power configs.\n- full_bore: available with AC (all sizes) and battery (all sizes listed in table 12: DN40-DN600, DN700, 750, 800, 900, 1000, 1050, 1100, 1200, 1350, 1400, 1500, 1600, 1650 — note DN1800 NOT in table 12 battery).\n- DN1650 also has flow rates: class 2, Q1 400, Q2 640, Q3 16000, Q4 20000 m³/h, Q3/Q1 = 40.\n\nHmm, is DN1800 full bore available in battery? Table 12's largest group is DN900–DN1650, so DN1800 battery is not listed. DN1800 appears only in Table 11 (full bore AC). So DN1800: full bore, AC mains only.\n\nBuilding per-model attributes with these details could be valuable but verbose. Given it's a fragment, maybe simpler: models list with model_id and minimal attributes (e.g., {\"bore_types\": [...], \"power_supplies\": [...]}). Let me do that — it's structured and faithful.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about what's most useful and correct. The schema: model_family.models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I'll provide:\n\n- DN40: attributes {bore_types: [\"reduced\",\"full\"], power_supplies: [\"AC mains\",\"battery/renewable energy\"]}\n- ... same for DN40–DN600 (all have both bores and both power options). Check: reduced bore tables include DN40–DN600 for both power types. Full bore tables include DN40–DN1800 (AC) and DN40–DN1650 (battery). So DN40–DN600: both bores, both powers. DN700–DN1200: full bore, both powers. DN1350–DN1650: full bore, both powers. DN1800: full bore, AC only.\n\nDN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650: full bore only; power both.\nDN1800: full bore only; AC only.\n\n- DN1650 additionally: flow rates etc.\n\nAlso characteristics.config_level: the indicating device characteristics vary by two axes: bore (reduced/full) × power supply (AC/battery). And the values are given per size-group. This is genuinely config-level (config axis: bore type and power supply). But the values also depend on size group... It's a 3-dimensional structure: size × bore × power → (indicating range max, indicating range min, VSI max). The matrix_tables capture this best.\n\nI'll emit matrix_tables:\n1. name: \"Table 9 – Indicating device, reduced bore, AC mains powered\", columns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_maximum\", \"indicating_range_minimum\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\"], column_units: [\"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"], rows with meter_size groups as strings like \"DN40, DN50, DN65\" (preserve grouping) — or expand each size to its own row? Expanding makes each row a single model: {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", indicating_range_maximum: 9999999, ...}. Expanding loses the grouping but adds clarity; the grouping is just a compression. I think expanding is better for machine use, and model_level characteristics can then be derived. But 4 tables × ~6 rows each, expanded maybe 15-29 rows per table... that's a lot of JSON. Alternatively keep grouped rows as given (meter_size as string \"DN40, DN50, DN65\"). The schema example for R137 rows used single size per row. For faithfulness and utility, I'll expand rows to individual sizes. Hmm, that makes big JSON but it's fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe better to preserve the table as-is (grouped) in matrix_tables, and additionally provide model_level characteristics for the per-size values by expanding. That doubles the data. Given the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" note about matrix detection, they want model_level populated. But here the varying attribute is per (size, bore, power) — config_level fits: attribute × config-axis matrix with condition strings.\n\nOption: use config_level entries like:\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\",\n  \"axis\": \"bore_type × power_supply\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40–DN65\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nThat's awkward. matrix_tables seem the cleanest. I'll do:\n- matrix_tables: 4 indicating device tables (expanded per size or grouped?) plus flow rate table.\n\nLet me decide: expand per size in matrix_tables rows, using meter_size as key, and add a \"bore_type\" and \"power_supply\" column? The schema's columns are free. I could combine all four tables into... no, keep 4 separate tables as named in the cert (Tables 9–12), each expanded per size.\n\nActually, preserving the original grouping is also faithful and compact. The instruction for matrix_tables: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Rows as object per row. I'll expand — machine readability wins; grouping is recoverable by identical values.\n\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety: should I also populate model_level characteristics from these tables? The R137 example populated model_level for qmax/qt/qmin AND matrix_tables. For consistency, I could populate model_level for indicating_range_max etc. But those values depend on config (bore, power), not just model. The distinguishing feature: for a given size, indicating range max is 9999999 mostly, 99999999 for large full-bore sizes, 999999999 for largest. It varies by size AND config. Putting in model_level would require model IDs like \"DN1650 (full bore, battery)\" — messy. matrix_tables is the right home. I'll put flow rates (Q1–Q4, ratio) in model_level for DN1650 since that's directly per-model, and keep indicating device stuff in matrix_tables only. Also maybe accuracy_class in model_level for DN1650 = 2. But class 1 exists for some sizes (per rev 6 history) — not in this fragment's tables. Only DN1650 row here shows class 2. I'll include accuracy_class model_level entry with just DN1650: 2? That could mislead (implying only DN1650 has class 2). Hmm. The fragment only shows this row; omit accuracy_class from model_level, keep it in the matrix table row. Also recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — class 1 mentioned in revision history (rev 6: \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\"), class 2 in the table