"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [{\"meter_size\": \"DN1650\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, \"q1\": 400, \"q2\": 640, \"q3\": 16000, \"q4\": 20000, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 40}],\n  \"description\": \"Flow rates Q1–Q4 can be freely chosen provided Q3 and Q3/Q1 are selected from clause 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values with Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values with Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 at least 40.\"\n}\n\nAnd model_level:\n- q1: [{model DN1650, 400 m³/h}] — hmm, is it worth it? For consistency with R137 example, yes. I'll add model_level entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 (and maybe accuracy_class). Values with unit_symbol m³/h.\n\nActually also note the constraints: Q1 and Q2 are minimum values; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values. So Q1=400 is a minimum allowed. Should value be {min: 400, max: null}? The table says \"Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values\". So the recorded Q1 for DN1650 is the minimum permissible. Hmm — I'd keep the nominal 400 in matrix table, and in model_level maybe note. To keep simple: value 400, and the description in matrix table covers semantics. Or model_level value {min:400,max:null}? That changes meaning. The certified values are these specific ones; the note says they \"can be freely chosen as long as\" ≥/≤ these. So the certified value is 400 with constraint it's a minimum. I'll keep 400 and put constraint info in the matrix_table description and maybe as footnote-like description. Keep it simple.\n\nNow the indicating device tables. Let me tabulate.\n\nTable 9 (Reduced bore – AC mains powered):\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: max 9999999, min 99999, VSI 0.0001\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 10 (Reduced bore – Battery/Renewable energy):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 11 (Full bore – AC mains):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 12 (Full bore – Battery/Renewable energy):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nDecimal conversion: 0,0001 → 0.0001; 0,001 → 0.001; 0,01 → 0.01; 0,1 → 0.1.\n\nNow, should rows be expanded per individual size? If expanded: Table 9: 15 rows; Table 10: 15 rows; Table 11: 29 rows; Table 12: 28 rows. Total 87 rows. That's a lot but okay. Alternatively keep grouped. Hmm. Given JSON verbosity concerns, maybe keep original grouped rows with meter_size as the group string. But then machine consumption is harder. The schema example rows: {\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...} — single size per row. I'll expand. It's long but correct.\n\nActually, let me think about total output size. 87 rows × ~80 chars ≈ 7KB just for rows. Plus the rest. Acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, a hybrid: 4 matrix tables with grouped rows as in source. I'll go with expanded per-size rows to align with the R137 example style, adding \"bore_type\" and \"power_supply\" columns? No — the tables are already separated by bore/power; the table name captures it. Adding columns would be redundant. Keep columns: meter_size, indicating_range_maximum, indicating_range_minimum, verification_scale_interval_maximum. Units: [mm, m³, m³, m³]. Meter size unit \"mm\" — DN sizes are nominal diameters in mm; the header says \"Meter size [mm]\". OK.\n\nNames: \"Table 9 – General characteristics of the indicating device – Reduced bore – AC mains powered\", etc. Shorter: \"Indicating device – reduced bore – AC mains powered (Table 9)\". I'll use descriptive names with table numbers.\n\nShould I also add config_level characteristics? Given matrix_tables capture it, maybe skip config_level to avoid duplication. But the schema emphasizes config_level for attribute × config-axis matrices. The indicating device characteristics vary along two config axes (bore, power) per size... The size dimension is model-level, config dims are bore and power. Honestly matrix_tables is the best fit; config_level entries would be redundant and clunky. I'll skip config_level. Hmm, but the grader may expect config_level usage. The rules say \"When attributes vary by configuration (not by model ...), use characteristics.config_level\". Here attributes vary by model (size) AND configuration (bore, power). The primary varying dimension across tables 9-12 is config. I could add a config_level entry for verification_scale_interval_maximum with axis \"bore_type and power_supply\", values with conditions like \"reduced bore / AC mains / DN40–DN65\". That duplicates matrix_tables. I'll include just matrix_tables — cleaner. Actually, let me add config_level for the two axes at least summarizing? No — risk of confusion. Decision: matrix_tables only.\n\nmodel_family.models: 29 entries with attributes:\n- bore_types available, power_supplies available.\n\nLet me define attributes:\nFor DN40–DN600: {\"bore_types\": [\"reduced\", \"full\"], \"power_supplies\": [\"AC mains\", \"battery/renewable energy\"]}\nFor DN700, 750, 800, 900, 1000, 1050, 1100, 1200, 1350, 1400, 1500, 1600, 1650: {\"bore_types\": [\"full\"], \"power_supplies\": [\"AC mains\", \"battery/renewable energy\"]}\nDN1800: {\"bore_types\": [\"full\"], \"power_supplies\": [\"AC mains\"]}\nDN1650 additionally: accuracy_class 2, flow rates — maybe fold into attributes? Could add \"accuracy_class\": 2, \"q1\": 400... but that duplicates matrix/model_level. I'll add accuracy_class: 2 to DN1650 attributes? It's in the flow table. Might as well keep model_level for flow attributes and leave DN1650 attributes same as peers. Hmm, I'll add accuracy_class to DN1650's attributes for completeness? It might imply others lack class 2 info. Keep attributes to bore/power only; flow data lives in matrix table + model_level.\n\nfamily_name: null (no explicit family name in fragment).\n\nmodels list order ascending DN.\n\nNow components: \n- {role: \"calculating and indicating device\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"], characteristics?} — from rev 9. The designation \"FEW45Y.Z\" with \"Mobile comms variant\". I'll record type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"].\n- {role: \"wireless module\", type_designations: [\"U200\"]} — from rev 10 \"change to U200 wireless module for global use\".\n\nBoth defensible. Include.\n\ncertified_type: category \"Water meter\". type_designations — omit (no explicit full type designation in fragment). description: omit? Could give \"Electromagnetic water meter\"? Not stated in fragment (AquaMaster is electromagnetic but not stated here). Omit description.\n\nmanufacturers: 4 entries with address_lines. \n- ABB Limited, [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"] — the address \"Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK)\". address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]. Fine.\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District\", \"Shanghai, 201319\", \"P.R. China\"].\n- ABB Inc., [\"125 E. County Line Road\", \"Warminster, PA 18974\", \"United States of America\"].\n- ABB India Limited, [\"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area\", \"Bengaluru – 560058\", \"Karnataka\", \"India\"].\n\nNote \"Bengaluru – 560058\" has an en dash; keep as text. Address lines are strings, no unit extraction needed.\n\nissuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\"}. Include.\n\nNow revision_history changes text: keep as given.\n- Initial / 2020-08-14 / \"First issue.\"\n- 1 / 2020-09-30 / \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600.\"\n- 2 / 2020-12-24 / \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\"\n- 3 / 2021-08-02 / \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to –25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"\n- 4 / 2021-08-26 / \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\"\n- 5 / 2021-11-23 / \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\"\n- 6 / 2022-05-20 / \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\"\n- 7 / 2022-08-30 / \"Software version updates due to addition of microprocessors.\"\n- 8 / 2023-07-03 / \"Software update\"\n- 9 / 2023-09-23 / \"Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device. Software update\"\n- 10 / 2024-02-26 / \"Software update and change to U200 wireless module for global use.\"\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2024-02-26\" (matches revision 10). I'll include. Also maybe \"revision\": \"10\" — not in schema; skip. Number: include revision? \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — keep number clean, and maybe number should be \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 10\"? Schema just says number. I'll use \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\".\n\npage_total: 13.\n\nNow build matrix_tables rows expanded.\n\nTable 9 rows (reduced bore, AC):\nDN40: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\nDN100: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nD